"Compares a normalized quantity across geographic regions by encoding its magnitude with ordered area fills.\n\nUse when: Show regional differences in rates, proportions or density. Compare administrative areas using a carefully defined denominator.\n\nAvoid when: Raw counts would mainly reflect region size or population, or boundaries do not match the data.\n\nVisual grammar: Region boundaries define the reporting units. Ordered lightness or a declared diverging scale encodes values. A legend states classes, units, denominator and missing-data treatment.\n\nBuild: Choose a rate appropriate to the geographic question. Match observations to consistent region boundaries and a common period. Select defensible class breaks and explain no-data regions separately.\n\nCheck: Numerators and denominators refer to the same population and period. Legend intervals are complete and unambiguous. Large geographic areas are not mistaken for larger statistical weight.\n\nPitfalls: Shading raw counts when rate differences are the intended story. Implying every location inside a region has its average value.\n\nExample: A regional access map shades districts by the share of residents within service reach, identifying the denominator and observation year.\n\nAlternatives: Proportional-symbol map, Dot-density map."
